; TEST: abort=1
;
; A TRAPPING `add` INSIDE A GROUP TREE TRAPS. `redinit u8 add arborea 4` with
; contributions 200, 100, 1, 1: the fourth contribution fills the group, the
; tree's first combine is 200 + 100 = 300, and 300 is not a `u8` -- so it
; aborts with kind 1, the shared `bfausr_trap`, exactly where the standalone
; `add u8 200 100` would (tests/ir/trap_add_u8.ir).
;
; WHY THIS IS THE DEFINITION AND NOT AN ARTEFACT. ssa-ir.md 2.6, as defined
; 2026-09-25: "integer ops trap exactly as the standalone opcode would". The
; reference lowering gets that for free rather than by re-implementing it --
; every combine and every fold goes through __bfa_emit_addsub_core, which is
; the routine the dispatcher's `add` reaches, so the normalise-and-compare at
; u8 (ssa-ir.md 2.2) is the same code and cannot drift. A reduction that
; accumulated at 64 bits and normalised once in `redfin` would answer 46
; (302 mod 256) and never trap, which is the mutation this fixture exists to
; catch.
;
; WHERE THE TRAP IS, precisely: not at the `contrib` that overflows an
; accumulator -- the accumulator is untouched until the group is complete --
; but at the first pairwise combine of the group tree. So three
; contributions of 200, 100, 1 abort NOTHING (the fixture would return 7),
; and the fourth is what makes the group, and the group is what traps. That
; is a real, observable property of `arborea` the definition has to state,
; and it is why the shape cannot be "flattened into ordinata" (ssa-ir.md
; 2.6): ordinata over the same four values traps at the SECOND contribution.
;
; If nothing traps the function returns 7.
;
; Mutation (run): the tree combine emitted as `addw` -> exit 7. The
; accumulator normalised only in `redfin` -> exit 7.
;
; CROSS-CHECKED THROUGH THE C BACKEND, 2026-09-27. This fixture carried
; `c-emit-exit=4` from the day it landed: the C backend refused the three
; reduction opcodes, so the differential phase could only check parity of a
; refusal. It carries the key no longer -- `backend_c/emit_c.inc` lowers
; `redinit`/`contrib`/`redfin`, and the four builds (gcc and clang, -O0 and
; -O2, every one under `-fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all`) are
; held to the SAME directive above as the reference's own run. The reference's
; verdict here is unchanged and has been since the day it landed; what is new
; is that three more toolchains have to agree with it.
;
; What this one cross-checks in particular: WHERE the trap is. The C lowering
; aborts inside `exsi_add_u`, called from the first pairwise combine of the
; group tree -- the same statement position the reference aborts at, and the
; same helper a standalone `add u8` would call. Measured 2026-09-27: all four
; builds abort with `exsecutor: abortus 1` and SIGILL, as the reference does.
; A C lowering that had accumulated at 64 bits would return 7 here, so the
; mutation above is now caught on both paths rather than one.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = iconst u8 200
%2 = iconst u8 100
%3 = iconst u8 1
%4 = redinit u8 add arborea 4
contrib %4 %1
contrib %4 %2
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
%5 = redfin u8 %4
%6 = iconst u8 7
ret %6
}
